<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lim (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lim is a river of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, about 220 km long, which rises near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plav">Plav</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and flows north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drina">Drina</a>. Its valley, with the towns of Bijelo Polje, Prijepolje and Priboj, has long been a route between the Adriatic hinterland and the central Balkans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lim-river.jpg" alt="Lim (river)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julian Nyča · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lim_and_Drina.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Lim is the longest right tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drina">Drina</a> and belongs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> drainage basin through the Drina, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Its drainage basin covers 5,968 square kilometres, and the river is not navigable. It flows out of Lake Plav to the north under the name Lim, passing Andrijevica, Berane and Bijelo Polje before it enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> between the villages of Dobrakovo and Gostun. Its two most important tributaries, the Uvac and the Mileševa, join it from the right in Serbia.</p><p>On the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> the river has carved a long gorge, the Kumanička klisura, between the mountains of Lisa and Ozren. It then runs through the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandzak">Sandžak</a>, past Brodarevo, Prijepolje and Priboj. After Priboj it turns north-west and enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> for a few kilometres, returns to Serbia, and crosses into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> again at Rudo. It empties into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drina">Drina</a> near the village of Međeđa, and its lowest section is flooded by the Višegrad Reservoir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Linguists including Franz Miklosich, Eqrem Çabej, Aleksandar Loma and Ivan Popović derive the name from the Albanian word lumë, meaning river. According to Loma, the name probably passed into South Slavic before the 10th or 12th century CE, depending on whether it was taken from lumë or from its dialect form lymë, and it was later passed back into Albanian. Popović holds that the name is not an ancient substrate but belongs to a pre-Slavic Albanian layer.</p><p>After the breakup of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1991 and 1992, the stretch of the Lim that enters and leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> left several Serbian villages physically cut off from the rest of the republic. The governments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> proposed an exchange of territory, but no agreement was reached. On 4 April 2004 a bus carrying Bulgarian tourists, 34 students and 16 adults returning to Svishtov from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>, plunged into the river near Gostun after the driver lost control on a bend. Twelve children died, and the 38 others were rescued with the help of local people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The fertile valley of the river is called Polimlje, meaning the Lim valley, and is made up of several gorges and depressions. It is divided into three large parts, the Montenegrin, Stari Vlah and Bosnian. The region is important for fruit growing and stockbreeding, and its road and railways, notably the Belgrade-Bar railway, link <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> and the Adriatic coast. Priboj is heavily industrialised, while Berane, Bijelo Polje and Prijepolje are smaller industrial centres. The Potpeć power station on the Lim and several stations on the Uvac produce electricity for Serbia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lim (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
